15:05:36 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Doctor Who\n1. The introduction of regeneration allows for the continuous ongoing of the Doctor Who story and character.', 'Doctor Who\n2. This process of regeneration, an essential element of the show, originated from the need to replace an actor when their health reached a point of compromise and recasting became necessary.', 'Doctor Who\n3. The concept of regeneration offers an effective solution for the show to navigate the challenge of dealing with departing actors and continuing the character development.', 'Doctor Who\n4. The term "regeneration" was not used until it was implemented with the Doctor\'s third regeneration on-screen.', 'Doctor Who\n5. The regeneration of the Doctor, as a form of renewal or change of appearance, has allowed for a smooth transition between different actors portraying the character.', "Doctor Who\n6. The process of regeneration facilitates the exploration of the character's past and future iterations, revealing the complexities and evolution of the character.", 'Doctor Who\n7. The ability to change actors and portray multiple versions of the Doctor challenges the traditional understanding of a single, consistent character arc, instead encouraging diverse interpretations of the persona.', "Doctor Who\n8. Regeneration also contributes to the show's enduring appeal, as it continually creates opportunities for new actors to infuse their own interpretation into the character.", "Doctor Who\n9. The concept of regeneration has become a central feature of Doctor Who, symbolizing the character's enduring legacy and constant evolution.", "Doctor Who\n10. This concept enables the show to progress through time and space, and also plays a role in the character's development, ultimately contributing to the longevity of the series.", 'Doctor Who\nProducers introduced the concept of regeneration to permit the recasting of the main character. This was first prompted by original star William Hartnell\'s poor health. The actual term "regeneration" was not initially conceived of until the Doctor\'s third on-screen regeneration however; Hartnell\'s Doctor had merely described undergoing a "renewal," and the Second Doctor underwent a "change of appearance".[citation needed] The device has allowed for the recasting of the actor various times in the show\'s history, as well as the depiction of alternative Doctors either from the Doctor\'s relative past or future.[citation needed]'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Doctor Who', 'context': 'Producers introduced the concept of regeneration to permit the recasting of the main character. This was first prompted by original star William Hartnell\'s poor health. The actual term "regeneration" was not initially conceived of until the Doctor\'s third on-screen regeneration however; Hartnell\'s Doctor had merely described undergoing a "renewal," and the Second Doctor underwent a "change of appearance".[citation needed] The device has allowed for the recasting of the actor various times in the show\'s history, as well as the depiction of alternative Doctors either from the Doctor\'s relative past or future.[citation needed]', 'question': 'Topic: Doctor Who\nWhat term is used to explain a change in the appearance of Doctor Who?', 'answer': 'regeneration'}, {'title': 'Doctor Who', 'context': 'Producers introduced the concept of regeneration to permit the recasting of the main character. This was first prompted by original star William Hartnell\'s poor health. The actual term "regeneration" was not initially conceived of until the Doctor\'s third on-screen regeneration however; Hartnell\'s Doctor had merely described undergoing a "renewal," and the Second Doctor underwent a "change of appearance".[citation needed] The device has allowed for the recasting of the actor various times in the show\'s history, as well as the depiction of alternative Doctors either from the Doctor\'s relative past or future.[citation needed]', 'question': 'Topic: Doctor Who\nWhen was the term "regeneration" first used?', 'answer': "the Doctor's third on-screen regeneration"}, {'title': 'Doctor Who', 'context': 'Producers introduced the concept of regeneration to permit the recasting of the main character. This was first prompted by original star William Hartnell\'s poor health. The actual term "regeneration" was not initially conceived of until the Doctor\'s third on-screen regeneration however; Hartnell\'s Doctor had merely described undergoing a "renewal," and the Second Doctor underwent a "change of appearance".[citation needed] The device has allowed for the recasting of the actor various times in the show\'s history, as well as the depiction of alternative Doctors either from the Doctor\'s relative past or future.[citation needed]', 'question': 'Topic: Doctor Who\nWhat first prompted the regeneration concept?', 'answer': "William Hartnell's poor health"}, {'title': 'Doctor Who', 'context': 'Producers introduced the concept of regeneration to permit the recasting of the main character. This was first prompted by original star William Hartnell\'s poor health. The actual term "regeneration" was not initially conceived of until the Doctor\'s third on-screen regeneration however; Hartnell\'s Doctor had merely described undergoing a "renewal," and the Second Doctor underwent a "change of appearance".[citation needed] The device has allowed for the recasting of the actor various times in the show\'s history, as well as the depiction of alternative Doctors either from the Doctor\'s relative past or future.[citation needed]', 'question': 'Topic: Doctor Who\nWhat term was used for the first regeneration?', 'answer': 'renewal'}, {'title': 'Doctor Who', 'context': 'Producers introduced the concept of regeneration to permit the recasting of the main character. This was first prompted by original star William Hartnell\'s poor health. The actual term "regeneration" was not initially conceived of until the Doctor\'