15:07:39 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Victoria and Albert Museum\n1. The Victoria and Albert Museum (V&A) is a highly significant institution in the world of decorative arts and design, known for its large collection of over 4.5 million objects. This indicates the V&A is a renowned and globally respected cultural landmark in its field.', "Victoria and Albert Museum\n2. Founded in 1852 by Queen Victoria and Prince Albert, the V&A has a rich history that places it among the oldest and most historic museums in the world. Its founding by a monarch and a royal figure speaks to the institution's prestigious and prestigious origins.", 'Victoria and Albert Museum\n3. The V&A is based in the Brompton area of London, in the Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea. The area is often referred to as "Albertopolis" in honour of Prince Albert, who was closely associated with many significant institutions in the district, including the Natural History Museum, the Science Museum, and the Royal Albert Hall. This suggests the V&A is part of a larger concentration of cultural and scientific institutions in the area.', "Victoria and Albert Museum\n4. The museum is an independent body, sponsored by the Department for Culture, Media and Sport. This implies the V&A has a high level of autonomy in its operations and governance, yet it still operates within the broader framework of the UK's cultural and governmental structures.", 'Victoria and Albert Museum\n5. Since its establishment in 2001, the V&A has been free to enter for all. This decision by the Department for Culture, Media and Sport has made the V&A accessible to all members of society, regardless of their ability to pay, which implies a strong commitment to universal accessibility and the public enjoyment of culture.', "Victoria and Albert Museum\n6. The V&A's collection spans a broad array of decorative arts and design objects from around the world and across time. This indicates the museum's mission is to be encyclopedic in its scope, representing as many diverse styles, periods, and cultures as possible within its vast collection.", "Victoria and Albert Museum\n7. As a national institution, the V&A's work likely carries a significant responsibility to preserve, exhibit, and research objects of cultural and historical significance. The museum serves both as an educational and inspirational resource for the public.", "Victoria and Albert Museum\n8. The V&A's presence in central London allows for easy public access and contributes to the city's cultural vibrancy and reputation as a global hub for art, history, and culture. This suggests the museum contributes to the city's tourist industry and cultural identity.", 'Victoria and Albert Museum\n9. Given its large size and free access, the V&A is a popular destination for both locals and international tourists. This high level of footfall implies a significant economic importance to the area, especially as a contributor to the local tourism industry.', 'Victoria and Albert Museum\n10. Finally, as a public institution, the V&A likely influences public perception and understanding of decorative arts and design. Its exhibitions, research, and public programs likely shape how people appreciate and comprehend these disciplines.', 'Victoria and Albert Museum\nThe Victoria and Albert Museum (often abbreviated as the V&A), London, is the world\'s largest museum of decorative arts and design, housing a permanent collection of over 4.5 million objects. It was founded in 1852 and named after Queen Victoria and Prince Albert. The V&A is located in the Brompton district of the Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea, in an area that has become known as "Albertopolis" because of its association with Prince Albert, the Albert Memorial and the major cultural institutions with which he was associated. These include the Natural History Museum, the Science Museum and the Royal Albert Hall. The museum is a non-departmental public body sponsored by the Department for Culture, Media and Sport. Like other national British museums, entrance to the museum has been free since 2001.'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Victoria and Albert Museum', 'context': 'The Victoria and Albert Museum (often abbreviated as the V&A), London, is the world\'s largest museum of decorative arts and design, housing a permanent collection of over 4.5 million objects. It was founded in 1852 and named after Queen Victoria and Prince Albert. The V&A is located in the Brompton district of the Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea, in an area that has become known as "Albertopolis" because of its association with Prince Albert, the Albert Memorial and the major cultural institutions with which he was associated. These include the Natural History Museum, the Science Museum and the Royal Albert Hall. The museum is a non-departmental public body sponsored by the Department for Culture, Media and Sport. Like other national British museums, entrance to the museum has been free since 2001.', 'question': 'Topic: Victoria and Albert Museum\nwhere is the Victoria and Albert Museum located?', 'answer': 'The V&A is located in the Brompton district of the Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea'}, {'title': 'Victoria and Albert Museum', 'context': 'The Victoria and Albert Museum (often abbreviated as the V&A), London, is the world\'s largest museum of decorative arts and design, housing a permanent collection of over 4.5 million objects. It was founded in 1852 and named after Queen Victoria and Prince Albert. The V&A is located in the Brompton district of the Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea, in an area that has become known as "Albertopolis" because of its association with Prince Albert, the Albert Memorial and the major cultural institutions with which he was associated. These include the Natural History Museum, the Science Museum and the Royal Albert Hall. The museum is a non-departmental public body sponsored by the Department for Culture, Media and Sport. Like other national British museums, entrance to the museum has been free since 2001.', 'question': 'Topic: Victoria and Albert Museum\nhow many permanent objects are located there?', 'answer': 'a permanent collection of over 4.5 million objects.'}, {'title': 'Victoria and Albert Museum', 'context': 'The Victoria and Albert Museum (often abbreviated as the V&A), London, is the world\'s largest museum of decorative arts and design, housing a permanent collection of over 4.5 million objects. It was founded in 1852 and named after Queen Victoria and Prince Albert. The V&A is located in the Brompton district of the Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea, in an area that has become known as "Albertopolis" because of its association with Prince Albert, the Albert Memorial and the major cultural institutions with which he was associated. These include the Natural History Museum, the Science Museum and the Royal Albert Hall. The museum is a non-departmental public body sponsored by the Department for Culture, Media and Sport. Like other national British museums, entrance to the museum has been free since 2001.', 'question': 'Topic: Victoria and Albert Museum\nwhen was the Victoria and Albert museum founded?', 'answer': 'It was founded in 1852'}, {'title': 'Victoria and Albert Museum', 'context': 'The Victoria and Albert Museum (often abbreviated as the V&A), London, is the world\'s largest museum of decorative arts and design, housing a permanent collection of over 4.5 million objects. It was founded in 1852 and named after Queen Victoria and Prince Albert. The V&A is located in the Brompton district of the Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea, in an area that has become known as "Albertopolis" because of its association with Prince Albert, the Albert Memorial and the major cultural institutions with which he was associated. These include the Natural History Museum, the Science Museum and the Royal Albert Hall. The museum is a non-departmental pu