15:11:20 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Huguenot\n1. The French attack on the Dutch Republic in 1672 prompted William III of Orange to form the League of Augsburg to oppose king Louis XIV and his policies.', 'Huguenot\n2. The Huguenots were drawn towards the Dutch Republic as an attractive country for exile after the revocation of the Edict of Nantes due to its Calvinist values and financial wealth.', 'Huguenot\n3. French-speaking Calvinist churches were also established in the Dutch Republic as a result of the Huguenot community.', 'Huguenot\n4. The Netherlands became a hub for Huguenots seeking a safe haven and to practice their religion after the revocation of the Edict of Nantes.', "Huguenot\n5. The Dutch Republic's position as a strong opponent of Louis XIV and his policies, as well as its tolerance of religious diversity, attracted many foreign religious minorities seeking refuge, such as the Huguenots.", 'Huguenot\n6. The Huguenots, being Calvinist, found a favorable environment in the Netherlands where they could practice their religion without persecution.', "Huguenot\n7. William III of Orange, who later became King of England, played a crucial role in defending the Netherlands against Louis XIV's aggression and in creating a safer environment for Huguenots to settle in the country.", 'Huguenot\n8. The revocation of the Edict of Nantes had significant effects on the religious and political dynamics of Europe during the time period, causing political strife and forcing religious minorities to seek refuge.', 'Huguenot\n9. The Dutch Republic, with its strong Calvinist values and financial power, emerged as a major player in the European political landscape during this time.', "Huguenot\n10. The Huguenots' presence in the Netherlands had a lasting impact on the cultural, religious, and economic landscape of the country, contributing to its richness and diversity.", "Huguenot\nLet's read the following passage and produce a list of implications derived directly or indirectly from the content.", 'Huguenot\nPassage:', 'Huguenot\nThis process enabled the Smiths to meet other prominent Jews of the era, including Meir Caro (later recognized as the chief rabbi of the Ottoman Empire) and his protege Obadiah ben Jacob Sforno. The Philo Greco-Roman Club, to which some members of the "synagogue of the Tyrians" belonged, met in a building in the Byzantine neighborhood of Neorion, and hosted many of the state\'s leaders in 1476. All this brought the Sephardic community into contact with elite members of the Christian minority in Rhodes, at a time when the republic was undergoing a traumatic experience—its final confrontation with Venice.', 'Huguenot\nImplications:', 'Huguenot\n1. The presence of prominent Jews such as Meir Caro and Obadiah ben Jacob Sforno in Rhodes allowed the Smiths, who were part of the "synagogue of the Tyrians," to meet and interact with notable members of the Jewish community.', 'Huguenot\n2. The Philo Greco-Roman Club, which the Smiths joined, was attended by leaders of the Christian community, and served as a platform for networking and forming connections between various groups in Rhodes.', 'Huguenot\n3. The fact that the Philo Greco-Roman Club was located in the Byzantine neighborhood of Neorion suggests that the city had a mix of different cultural and religious influences, providing opportunities for integration and interaction among its diverse populations.', 'Huguenot\n4. The importance of the Philo Greco-Roman Club as a center for leaders of the state implies that it had a significant role in politics and governance during the time, as it hosted many prominent figures.', 'Huguenot\n5. The encounter between the Sephardic community and elite members of the Christian minority in Rhodes reflects the complex interplay of different religious and cultural groups within the broader society of Rhodes.', 'Huguenot\n6. The meeting of the Smiths with influential figures from the Jewish community and the Philo Greco-Roman Club highlights the dynamic and interconnected nature of the social network in Rhodes, where people from different backgrounds came together for discussion and interaction.', 'Huguenot\n7. The final confrontation with Venice, as a traumatic event for Rhodes, may have further impacted the relationships and alliances among the different groups, possibly leading to a restructuring of the social and political landscape.', 'Huguenot\n8. The association of the Smiths with the "synagogue of the Tyrians" demonstrates the presence of a significant Jewish population in Rhodes during the time period, suggesting a thriving Jewish community.', 'Huguenot\n9. The involvement of the Sephardic community with influential members of the Christian minority may have led to mutual understanding and respect, as well as an exchange of ideas and knowledge between the two groups.', 'Huguenot\n10. The experience of the Smiths attending the meetings of the Philo Greco-Roman Club and hosting leaders from various groups in Rhodes provides insights into the social interactions and cultural exchanges that took place during this period, shedding light on the diversity of the city.', "Huguenot\nLet's read the following passage and produce a list of implications derived directly or indirectly from the content.", 'Huguenot\nPassage:', 'Huguenot\nAlso, a virtual reality apparatus allows visitors to take a brief visual tour of what space might look like from another planet. Computer and holography technicians program all the scenes and effects. It seems that the impact of the programmed experience is increased by the knowledge that it is real, not a fake, and will stand up to examination. Reality is almost meaningless for visitors who have been inside it: after walking through the installation, trying on the helmet and gloves, putting on the space suits, the visitors can run around on the surfaces of a planet without any real effort on their part, since they are suspended a few feet off the floor. The computer can put out a rainbow of color if one enters a four-dimensional space, and magnify a particular area so it becomes a strong hologram to photograph.', 'Huguenot\nImplications:', 'Huguenot\n1. The virtual reality apparatus at the space exhibit creates a convincing and interactive experience for visitors, allowing them to visualize space and simulate the experience of exploring other planets.', 'Huguenot\n2. The computer and holography technicians involved in programming the scenes and effects ensure that the virtual reality environment is realistic and will withstand examination.', 'Huguenot\n3. The perceived authenticity and realism of the virtual reality experience increases the impact it has on visitors.', 'Huguenot\n4. Visitors to the space exhibit can immerse themselves in a simulated environment where they can explore different planets and experience the sensation of walking in space.', 'Huguenot\n5. The virtual reality helmet and gloves provide an immersive and interactive experience for the visitors, allowing them to feel like they are physically present in a space environment.', 'Huguenot\n6. The suspended surface on which visitors can run around on adds to the feeling of weightlessness and enhances the immersive experience.', 'Huguenot\n7. The computer technology can create dynamic and changing environments, including the ability to explore four-dimensional spaces and produce strong holograms to photograph.', 'Huguenot\n8. The exhibit offers a unique opportunity for visitors to learn and experience aspects of space exploration that are otherwise impossible in a real-world setting.', 'Huguenot\n9. The virtual reality apparatus enables visitors to explore a variety of environments and scenarios within space, providing a comprehensive understanding of the space environment.', 'Huguenot\n10. The exhibit demonstrates the power of technology in creating realistic and impactful experiences, transforming traditional learning into an immersive and engaging activity.', "Huguenot\nLet's read the following passage and produce a list of implications derived directly or indirectly from the content.", 'Huguenot\nPassage:', 'Huguenot\nThe preceding images are meant as intuitive introductions to the three-dimensional space we know. Of course, with the modern advent of space travel, these two-dimensional images of Earth are becoming much less useful. As Huygens confirmed in the seventeenth century, the Earth is not flat but spherical. It is easy for us to comprehend this – by travelling on planes. But even so, the traveller can still only experience the world from two dimensions. To really experience three dimensions, you have to live within the space itself. You have to become familiar with the beauty, randomness and strangeness of that space – as New Brunswickers have been.', 'Huguenot\nImplications:', 'Huguenot\n1. The development of space travel and the modern ability to physically explore space has made it increasingly important to understand three-dimensional space beyond the two-dimensional images we commonly see.', "Huguenot\n2. The Earth's spherical shape has been confirmed through space travel, specifically by missions such as the Huygens mission in the seventeenth century.", 'Huguenot\n3. Despite the possibility to experience the Earth from the perspective of three dimensions, individuals can still only experience it from a two-dimensional perspective, primarily through virtual and imaging technologies.', 'Huguenot\n4. In order to truly understand and appreciate the complexity and uniqueness of three-dimensional space, individuals must immerse themselves within it and live in that environment.', 'Huguenot\n5. The ability to become familiar with the beauty, randomness, and strangeness of three-dimensional space, as experienced by New Brunswickers, highlights the importance of direct exposure and exploration.', "Huguenot\n6. The realization of the Earth's three-dimensional nature emphasizes the need for a deeper understanding and appreciation of the world in which we live.", 'Huguenot\n7. The growing importance of three-dimensional space in our lives underscores the need for ongoing advancements in technology, exploration, and education to further our comprehension of this complex and fascinating space.', 'Huguenot\n8. The comprehension of three-dimensional space goes beyond merely understanding it intellectually; it involves a sense of personal connection and experience to truly appreciate its intricacies.', "Huguenot\n9. The recognition of the Earth's spherical shape and its influence on our perspective of space underscores the importance of constantly revising and expanding our understanding of the world around us.", 'Huguenot\n10. The exploration of three-dimensional space, both physically and intellectually, plays a crucial role in shaping our collective knowledge and perspective on the universe, helping us comprehend and appreciate its intricacies and mysteries.', "Huguenot\nLet's read the following passage and produce a list of implications derived directly or indirectly from the content.", 'Huguenot\nPassage:', 'Huguenot\nWe would like to encourage the amendment of the law to allow the aboriginal nations more self-determination, including the ability to exercise responsibilities over the lands in the Arctic governed by the current Royal Canadian Geographical Society map of Canada. The federal government will work with those aboriginal nations and other interested parties to resolve the question of replacing the current flag with one that is appropriate for the Arctic, one that reflects what the area is really like and which truly represents those who live there.', 'Huguenot\nHe called', 'Huguenot\nStadtholder William III of Orange, who later became King of England, emerged as the strongest opponent of king Louis XIV after the French attacked the Dutch Republic in 1672. William formed the League of Augsburg as a coalition to oppose Louis and the French state. Consequently, many Huguenots considered the wealthy and Calvinist Dutch Republic, which led the opposition to Louis XIV, as the most attractive country for exile after the revocation of the Edict of Nantes. They also found many French-speaking Calvinist churches there.'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Huguenot', 'context': 'Stadtholder William III of Orange, who later became King of England, emerged as the strongest opponent of king Louis XIV after the French attacked the Dutch Republic in 1672. William formed the League of Augsburg as a coalition to oppose Louis and the French state. Consequently, many Huguenots considered the wealthy and Calvinist Dutch Republic, which led the opposition to Louis XIV, as the most attractive country for exile after the revocation of the Edict of Nantes. They also found many French-speaking Calvinist churches there.', 'question': "Topic: Huguenot\nWho was Louis XIV's main rival?", 'answer': 'William III of Orange'}, {'title': 'Huguenot', 'context': 'Stadtholder William III of Orange, who later became King of England, emerged as the strongest opponent of king Louis XIV after the French attacked the Dutch Republic in 1672. William formed the League of Augsburg as a coalition to oppose Louis and the French state. Consequently, many Huguenots considered the wealthy and Calvinist Dutch Republic, which led the opposition to Louis XIV, as the most attractive country for exile after the revocation of the Edict of Nantes. They also found many French-speaking Calvinist churches there.', 'question': 'Topic: Huguenot\nWilliam would eventually gain what throne?', 'answer': 'King of England'}, {'title': 'Huguenot', 'context': 'Stadtholder William III of Orange, who later became King of England, emerged as the strongest opponent of king Louis XIV after the French attacked the Dutch Republic in 1672. William formed the League of Augsburg as a coalition to oppose Louis and the French state. Consequently, many Huguenots considered the wealthy and Calvinist Dutch Republic, which led the opposition to Louis XIV, as the most attractive country for exile after the revocation of the Edict of Nantes. They also found many French-speaking Calvinist churches there.', 'question': "Topic: Huguenot\nWhat coalition rose up to oppose Louis XIV's France?", 'answer': 'League of Augsburg'}, {'title': 'Huguenot', 'context': 'Stadtholder William III of Orange, who later became King of England, emerged as the strongest opponent of king Louis XIV after the French attacked the Dutch Republic in 1672. William formed the League of Augsburg as a coalition to oppose Louis and the French state. Consequently, many Huguenots considered the wealthy and Calvinist Dutch Republic, which led the opposition to Louis XIV, as the most attractive country for exile after the revocation of the Edict of Nantes. They also found many French-speaking Calvinist churches there.', 'question': 'Topic: Huguenot\nWith what European country did the Huguenots feel kinship for emigration to?', 'answer': 'Dutch Republic'}, {'title': 'Huguenot', 'context': 'Stadtholder William III of Orange, who later became King of England, emerged as the strongest opponent of king Louis XIV after the French attacked the Dutch Republic in 1672. William formed the League of Augsburg as a coalition to oppose Louis and the French state. Consequently, many Huguenots considered the wealthy and Calvinist Dutch Republic, which led the opposition to Louis XIV, as the most attractive country for exile after the revocation of the Edict of Nantes. They also found many French-speaking Calvinist churches there.', 'question': 'Topic: Huguenot\nWhen did France and the Dutch fight in the 17th century?', 'answer': '1672'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs': 10, 'finetune_lr': 0.001, 'batch_size': 1, 'gradient_accumulation_steps': 1, 'end_mask_substring': ''}
