15:17:20 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ["Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\n1. Chairman Joe Barton's demand for records from Mann, Bradley, and Hughes raises concerns about the motive behind the request and the potential intimidation of scientists.", 'Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\n2. The involvement of Rep. Joe Barton and Ed Whitfield, both with regulatory oversight in climate research, indicates that their actions are politically motivated.', "Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\n3. Sherwood Boehlert's criticism of Barton's investigation and his suggestion that it could be part of a larger pattern of intimidation for scientists, suggests that there may be a broader trend of attempts to undermine climate research.", "Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\n4. The request for personal information about the scientists' finances and careers might be seen as an attempt to discredit them or shift focus from the science to their personal lives.", "Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\n5. The National Academy of Sciences' decision to set up a special investigation to examine the claims made by Barton indicates that the request for records was considered important and significant.", "Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\n6. The National Research Council's report supporting the basic accuracy of Mann, Bradley, and Hughes' research suggests that there might be limited consensus among scientists on climate change topics.", "Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\n7. Mann, Bradley, and Hughes' letter to Nature following the Barton investigation shows that they view their work as ongoing and suggesting the need for more research to improve their conclusions.", 'Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\n8. The statement from the National Research Council confirms that there were some statistical issues in the original article, but that they did not significantly alter the data or conclusions presented.', "Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\n9. Mann, Bradley, and Hughes' continued research into climate change acknowledges the importance of the subject and the ongoing nature of scientific inquiry.", 'Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\n10. The motives behind the Barton investigation remain unclear and may suggest a larger, political agenda.', "Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\nLet's read the following passage and produce a list of implications derived directly or indirectly from the content.", 'Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\nPassage:', 'Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\nMann was honored on March 19, 2008, at the second annual Science & Public Policy Award of the Science & Public Policy Institute, a conservative think tank based in Maryland. This award went to him for his role in compiling the Mann, Bradley, and Hughes 1998 chart, which the Institute claims "confirmed that global warming is occurring, but more importantly disproved the idea that this problem was unprecedented, showing a notable global temperature spike in the 20th century." Opponents of this idea have expressed deep concern about the end of the Little Ice Age causing a rapid increase in global temperatures, and this award is seen as directly counter to such opinions.', 'Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\nImplications:', "Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\n1. The award from the Science & Public Policy Institute represents recognition for Mann's work in compiling the Mann, Bradley, and Hughes 1998 chart and challenging the idea of unprecedented global warming.", "Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\n2. The award may be viewed as an endorsement of the validity of Mann's research, and a counterpoint to scientific claims of a rapid increase in global temperatures at the end of the Little Ice Age.", 'Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\n3. The award could be interpreted as reflecting a political agenda that supports the positions of the Science & Public Policy Institute, who are a conservative think tank.', "Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\n4. Mann's award may be seen as controversy-inducing, both in the scientific community and in broader political circles.", "Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\n5. The Science & Public Policy Institute's mission is to provide conservative perspectives on science and public policy, so their selection of Mann for this award is likely to align with their policy goals.", "Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\n6. Opposition to the idea of an unprecedented global warming spike has expressed deep concern, implying that it's a view that needs addressing or clarifying.", "Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\n7. Mann's work, the award, and the associated controversy shed light on the importance of scientific research and its impact on public opinion and policy.", "Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\n8. The Science & Public Policy Institute's actions may be motivated by a desire to support conservative ideologies rather than scientific consensus.", "Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\n9. It's possible that the award was a deliberate attempt to influence public opinion and discredit Mann's research by associating it with a political agenda.", 'Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\n10. The award serves as an example of how scientific research can become politicized, and highlights the need for scientists to remain transparent about their methods and findings to maintain credibility.', "Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\nLet's read the following passage and produce a list of implications derived directly or indirectly from the content.", 'Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\nPassage:', "Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\nFollowing their loss to the Patriots, the Broncos headed to Sports Authority Field for their Week 13 home opener against the Miami Dolphins. In the first quarter, the Broncos drew first blood as RB Ronnie Hillman got a 1-yard TD run. However, the Dolphins responded with QB Ryan Tannehill completing a 2-yard TD pass to WR Brian Hartline. In the second quarter, Miami took the lead with Tannehill completing a 57-yard TD pass to TE Jordan Cameron. The Broncos answered back with QB Peyton Manning completing a 50-yard TD pass to WR Wes Welker, along with RB C. J. Anderson getting a 2-yard TD run. The Dolphins took a 24-21 lead in the third quarter with kicker Dan Carpenter nailing a 48-yard field goal. Denver responded in the fourth quarter as Anderson got a 37-yard TD run to put the game out of reach. The Dolphins tried to come back in the final seconds, but Carpenter's 65-yard field goal attempt hit the left upright and bounced off the ground for the win. With the win, the Broncos improved to 9-3, and clinched a playoff berth for the 11th straight season. Also, the Broncos tied the team record for wins in a season with 10. Additionally, the Broncos became the fourth team in NFL history to reach 10 wins by the middle of the season (along with the 1974 Dolphins, 2013 49ers, and 2016 Eagles).", 'Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\nImplications:', "Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\n1. The Broncos' victory over the Miami Dolphins in Week 13 was their 9th win of the season.", 'Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\n2. Denver won their 11th straight playoff berth, continuing a long-standing streak.', 'Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\n3. The team tied their own record for the most wins in a season (10).', 'Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\n4. The Broncos became the fourth NFL team in history to achieve 10 wins by the middle of the season.', 'Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\n5. The victory provided the Broncos a significant advantage going into the remainder of their season.', "Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\n6. It was a notable achievement for the team's fans, who got to witness the team's historic accomplishments.", "Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\n7. Peyton Manning's performance, including the 50-yard TD pass to Wes Welker, boosted his legacy within the franchise.", "Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\n8. The Broncos effectively staved off an opponent known for their speed and playmaking ability, showing the team's resiliency and tactical ability.", "Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\n9. In the 4th quarter, Denver's C.J. Anderson became the focus of the offensive strategy, providing crucial momentum for the team.", "Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\n10. The game showcased Peyton Manning's ability to step up in high-pressure situations, demonstrating his leadership qualities and overall performance as a quarterback.", 'Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\nOn 23 June 2005, Rep. Joe Barton, chairman of the House Committee on Energy and Commerce wrote joint letters with Ed Whitfield, Chairman of the Subcommittee on Oversight and Investigations demanding full records on climate research, as well as personal information about their finances and careers, from Mann, Bradley and Hughes. Sherwood Boehlert, chairman of the House Science Committee, said this was a "misguided and illegitimate investigation" apparently aimed at intimidating scientists, and at his request the U.S. National Academy of Sciences arranged for its National Research Council to set up a special investigation. The National Research Council\'s report agreed that there were some statistical failings, but these had little effect on the graph, which was generally correct. In a 2006 letter to Nature, Mann, Bradley, and Hughes pointed out that their original article had said that "more widespread high-resolution data are needed before more confident conclusions can be reached" and that the uncertainties were "the point of the article".'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change', 'context': 'On 23 June 2005, Rep. Joe Barton, chairman of the House Committee on Energy and Commerce wrote joint letters with Ed Whitfield, Chairman of the Subcommittee on Oversight and Investigations demanding full records on climate research, as well as personal information about their finances and careers, from Mann, Bradley and Hughes. Sherwood Boehlert, chairman of the House Science Committee, said this was a "misguided and illegitimate investigation" apparently aimed at intimidating scientists, and at his request the U.S. National Academy of Sciences arranged for its National Research Council to set up a special investigation. The National Research Council\'s report agreed that there were some statistical failings, but these had little effect on the graph, which was generally correct. In a 2006 letter to Nature, Mann, Bradley, and Hughes pointed out that their original article had said that "more widespread high-resolution data are needed before more confident conclusions can be reached" and that the uncertainties were "the point of the article".', 'question': 'Topic: Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\nWho was the chairman of the House Committee on Energy and Commerce?', 'answer': 'Rep. Joe Barton'}, {'title': 'Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change', 'context': 'On 23 June 2005, Rep. Joe Barton, chairman of the House Committee on Energy and Commerce wrote joint letters with Ed Whitfield, Chairman of the Subcommittee on Oversight and Investigations demanding full records on climate research, as well as personal information about their finances and careers, from Mann, Bradley and Hughes. Sherwood Boehlert, chairman of the House Science Committee, said this was a "misguided and illegitimate investigation" apparently aimed at intimidating scientists, and at his request the U.S. National Academy of Sciences arranged for its National Research Council to set up a special investigation. The National Research Council\'s report agreed that there were some statistical failings, but these had little effect on the graph, which was generally correct. In a 2006 letter to Nature, Mann, Bradley, and Hughes pointed out that their original article had said that "more widespread high-resolution data are needed before more confident conclusions can be reached" and that the uncertainties were "the point of the article".', 'question': 'Topic: Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\nWho was Chairman of the Subcommittee on Oversight and Investigations?', 'answer': 'Ed Whitfield'}, {'title': 'Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change', 'context': 'On 23 June 2005, Rep. Joe Barton, chairman of the House Committee on Energy and Commerce wrote joint letters with Ed Whitfield, Chairman of the Subcommittee on Oversight and Investigations demanding full records on climate research, as well as personal information about their finances and careers, from Mann, Bradley and Hughes. Sherwood Boehlert, chairman of the House Science Committee, said this was a "misguided and illegitimate investigation" apparently aimed at intimidating scientists, and at his request the U.S. National Academy of Sciences arranged for its National Research Council to set up a special investigation. The National Research Council\'s report agreed that there were some statistical failings, but these had little effect on the graph, which was generally correct. In a 2006 letter to Nature, Mann, Bradley, and Hughes pointed out that their original article had said that "more widespread high-resolution data are needed before more confident conclusions can be reached" and that the uncertainties were "the point of the article".', 'question': 'Topic: Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\nWhen did Barton and Whitfield demand climate research records?', 'answer': '23 June 2005'}, {'title': 'Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change', 'context': 'On 23 June 2005, Rep. Joe Barton, chairman of the House Committee on Energy and Commerce wrote joint letters with Ed Whitfield, Chairman of the Subcommittee on Oversight and Investigations demanding full records on climate research, as well as personal information about their finances and careers, from Mann, Bradley and Hughes. Sherwood Boehlert, chairman of the House Science Committee, said this was a "misguided and illegitimate investigation" apparently aimed at intimidating scientists, and at his request the U.S. National Academy of Sciences arranged for its National Research Council to set up a special investigation. The National Research Council\'s report agreed that there were some statistical failings, but these had little effect on the graph, which was generally correct. In a 2006 letter to Nature, Mann, Bradley, and Hughes pointed out that their original article had said that "more widespread high-resolution data are needed before more confident conclusions can be reached" and that the uncertainties were "the point of the article".', 'question': 'Topic: Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\nWho was chairman of the House Science Committee?', 'answer': 'Sherwood Boehlert'}, {'title': 'Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change', 'context': 'On 23 June 2005, Rep. Joe Barton, chairman of the House Committee on Energy and Commerce wrote joint letters with Ed Whitfield, Chairman of the Subcommittee on Oversight and Investigations demanding full records on climate research, as well as personal information about their finances and careers, from Mann, Bradley and Hughes. Sherwood Boehlert, chairman of the House Science Committee, said this was a "misguided and illegitimate investigation" apparently aimed at intimidating scientists, and at his request the U.S. National Academy of Sciences arranged for its National Research Council to set up a special investigation. The National Research Council\'s report agreed that there were some statistical failings, but these had little effect on the graph, which was generally correct. In a 2006 letter to Nature, Mann, Bradley, and Hughes pointed out that their original article had said that "more widespread high-resolution data are needed before more confident conclusions can be reached" and that the uncertainties were "the point of the article".', 'question': 'Topic: Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\nWho said Barton\'s investigation was "misguided and illegitimate"?', 'answer': 'Sherwood Boehlert'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs': 10, 'finetune_lr': 0.001, 'batch_size': 1, 'gradient_accumulation_steps': 1, 'end_mask_substring': ''}
