15:44:44 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ["Victoria and Albert Museum\n1. The Victoria and Albert Museum has a significant collection of Meissen porcelain, one of Europe's earliest porcelain factories.", 'Victoria and Albert Museum\n2. Among the finest examples of Meissen porcelain are the Meissen Vulture and the Möllendorff Dinner Service.', 'Victoria and Albert Museum\n3. The museum has an extensive collection of ceramics from the Manufacture nationale de Sèvres, spanning the 18th and 19th centuries.', 'Victoria and Albert Museum\n4. The collection of 18th-century British porcelain is the largest and finest in the world.', 'Victoria and Albert Museum\n5. Examples from every British porcelain factory are represented, with the collections of Chelsea porcelain and Worcester Porcelain being particularly notable.', 'Victoria and Albert Museum\n6. The museum also has a major collection of 19th-century British porcelain.', "Victoria and Albert Museum\n7. The Salting Bequest in 1909 significantly enriched the museum's collection of Chinese and Japanese ceramics.", 'Victoria and Albert Museum\n8. The combined collections of Chinese and Japanese ceramics form the finest collection of East Asian pottery and porcelain in the world, including Kakiemon ware.', "Victoria and Albert Museum\nWell represented in the collection is Meissen porcelain, from the first factory in Europe to discover the Chinese method of making porcelain. Among the finest examples are the Meissen Vulture from 1731 and the Möllendorff Dinner Service, designed in 1762 by Frederick II the Great. Ceramics from the Manufacture nationale de Sèvres are extensive, especially from the 18th and 19th centuries. The collection of 18th-century British porcelain is the largest and finest in the world. Examples from every factory are represented, the collections of Chelsea porcelain and Worcester Porcelain being especially fine. All the major 19th-century British factories are also represented. A major boost to the collections was the Salting Bequest made in 1909, which enriched the museum's stock of Chinese and Japanese ceramics. This bequest forms part of the finest collection of East Asian pottery and porcelain in the world, including Kakiemon ware."], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Victoria and Albert Museum', 'context': "Well represented in the collection is Meissen porcelain, from the first factory in Europe to discover the Chinese method of making porcelain. Among the finest examples are the Meissen Vulture from 1731 and the Möllendorff Dinner Service, designed in 1762 by Frederick II the Great. Ceramics from the Manufacture nationale de Sèvres are extensive, especially from the 18th and 19th centuries. The collection of 18th-century British porcelain is the largest and finest in the world. Examples from every factory are represented, the collections of Chelsea porcelain and Worcester Porcelain being especially fine. All the major 19th-century British factories are also represented. A major boost to the collections was the Salting Bequest made in 1909, which enriched the museum's stock of Chinese and Japanese ceramics. This bequest forms part of the finest collection of East Asian pottery and porcelain in the world, including Kakiemon ware.", 'question': 'Topic: Victoria and Albert Museum\nIn which year are the Meissen Vulture in the V&A collection dated?', 'answer': '1731'}, {'title': 'Victoria and Albert Museum', 'context': "Well represented in the collection is Meissen porcelain, from the first factory in Europe to discover the Chinese method of making porcelain. Among the finest examples are the Meissen Vulture from 1731 and the Möllendorff Dinner Service, designed in 1762 by Frederick II the Great. Ceramics from the Manufacture nationale de Sèvres are extensive, especially from the 18th and 19th centuries. The collection of 18th-century British porcelain is the largest and finest in the world. Examples from every factory are represented, the collections of Chelsea porcelain and Worcester Porcelain being especially fine. All the major 19th-century British factories are also represented. A major boost to the collections was the Salting Bequest made in 1909, which enriched the museum's stock of Chinese and Japanese ceramics. This bequest forms part of the finest collection of East Asian pottery and porcelain in the world, including Kakiemon ware.", 'question': 'Topic: Victoria and Albert Museum\nWho designed the  Möllendorff Dinner Service?', 'answer': 'Frederick II the Great'}, {'title': 'Victoria and Albert Museum', 'context': "Well represented in the collection is Meissen porcelain, from the first factory in Europe to discover the Chinese method of making porcelain. Among the finest examples are the Meissen Vulture from 1731 and the Möllendorff Dinner Service, designed in 1762 by Frederick II the Great. Ceramics from the Manufacture nationale de Sèvres are extensive, especially from the 18th and 19th centuries. The collection of 18th-century British porcelain is the largest and finest in the world. Examples from every factory are represented, the collections of Chelsea porcelain and Worcester Porcelain being especially fine. All the major 19th-century British factories are also represented. A major boost to the collections was the Salting Bequest made in 1909, which enriched the museum's stock of Chinese and Japanese ceramics. This bequest forms part of the finest collection of East Asian pottery and porcelain in the world, including Kakiemon ware.", 'question': 'Topic: Victoria and Albert Museum\nIn which year was Möllendorff Dinner Service designed?', 'answer': '1762'}, {'title': 'Victoria and Albert Museum', 'context': "Well represented in the collection is Meissen porcelain, from the first factory in Europe to discover the Chinese method of making porcelain. Among the finest examples are the Meissen Vulture from 1731 and the Möllendorff Dinner Service, designed in 1762 by Frederick II the Great. Ceramics from the Manufacture nationale de Sèvres are extensive, especially from the 18th and 19th centuries. The collection of 18th-century British porcelain is the largest and finest in the world. Examples from every factory are represented, the collections of Chelsea porcelain and Worcester Porcelain being especially fine. All the major 19th-century British factories are also represented. A major boost to the collections was the Salting Bequest made in 1909, which enriched the museum's stock of Chinese and Japanese ceramics. This bequest forms part of the finest collection of East Asian pottery and porcelain in the world, including Kakiemon ware.", 'question': 'Topic: Victoria and Albert Museum\nIn which year was the Salting Bequest made?', 'answer': '1909'}, {'title': 'Victoria and Albert Museum', 'context': "Well represented in the collection is Meissen porcelain, from the first factory in Europe to discover the Chinese method of making porcelain. Among the finest examples are the Meissen Vulture from 1731 and the Möllendorff Dinner Service, designed in 1762 by Frederick II the Great. Ceramics from the Manufacture nationale de Sèvres are extensive, especially from the 18th and 19th centuries. The collection of 18th-century British porcelain is the largest and finest in the world. Examples from every factory are represented, the collections of Chelsea porcelain and Worcester Porcelain being especially fine. All the major 19th-century British factories are also represented. A major boost to the collections was the Salting Bequest made in 1909, which enriched the museum's stock of Chinese and Japanese ceramics. This bequest forms part of the finest collection of East Asian pottery and porcelain in the world, including Kakiemon ware.", 'question': 'Topic: Victoria and Albert Museum\nWhich part of the V&A collection did the Salting Bequest enhanced?', 'answer': 'Chinese and Japanese ceramics'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs': 10, 'fi