15:59:35 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['United Methodist Church\n1. Prevenient grace is available to all people, regardless of their background or situation.', 'United Methodist Church\n2. It enables individuals to love and fosters a desire to have a relationship with God.', "United Methodist Church\n3. This grace assists in overcoming the effects of sin, both in terms of willingness to accept God's salvation and in understanding one's own sinfulness.", 'United Methodist Church\n4. It initiates the process of personal spiritual growth, leading individuals towards a closer relationship with God through Jesus Christ.', 'United Methodist Church\n5. Prevenient grace underscores the idea that salvation is a personal, individual choice, allowing each person to freely decide whether to accept or reject it.', "United Methodist Church\n6. This concept emphasizes God's desire for every individual to choose a life of faith and forgiveness, rather than being condemned by their past sins.", "United Methodist Church\n7. By acknowledging one's own sinfulness and the offer of salvation, individuals can gain a deeper sense of humility and dependence on God.", "United Methodist Church\n8. Prevenient grace represents God's desire for humanity to recognize the need for redemption and healing, which can be found in Jesus Christ.", "United Methodist Church\n9. This grace provides the foundation for further spiritual development and growth, enabling individuals to actively participate in the process of choosing and embracing God's salvation.", "United Methodist Church\n10. The concept of prevenient grace highlights the belief that individuals have a choice in the matter of accepting God's salvation, which requires free will and a genuine desire to turn to God.", "United Methodist Church\nLet's read the following passage and produce a list of implications derived directly or indirectly from the content.", 'United Methodist Church\nPassage:', 'United Methodist Church\nUnited Methodist Church', 'United Methodist Church\nAlthough the ordination of women was controversial at the time of the Great Awakening in the 18th century, it had been occurring irregularly in some cases for centuries prior to the breaking away of the Methodists from the Church of England. Church founder John Wesley preferred that women serve as local preachers. He ordained his first woman deacon, Anna N. Harrison, in 1784, and his first woman preacher, Sarah Chedzoy, in 1786. He denied women "the episcopal office" until the twentieth century, but in other cases, he said they could "authorize" others to do tasks they wanted done, such as visiting widows and orphans. Wesley directed his female preachers, including Harriet Chang, to avoid preaching to mixed-gender congregations, until females, "in the mass", become "qualified to attend to divine services as the men do".', 'United Methodist Church\nImplications:', 'United Methodist Church\n1. The ordination of women faced controversy during the Great Awakening in the 18th century.', 'United Methodist Church\n2. Women have been ordaining themselves in some cases for centuries before the Methodist separation from the Church of England.', 'United Methodist Church\n3. John Wesley was open to the idea of women serving as local preachers.', 'United Methodist Church\n4. Wesley ordained his first woman deacon, Anna N. Harrison, in 1784, and his first woman preacher, Sarah Chedzoy, in 1786.', 'United Methodist Church\n5. Women were denied the episcopal office until the 20th century in the United Methodist Church.', 'United Methodist Church\n6. Wesley allowed women to authorize others to perform tasks, such as visiting widows and orphans.', 'United Methodist Church\n7. He advised his female preachers, including Harriet Chang, to avoid preaching to mixed-gender congregations until females were qualified to attend divine services like men.', 'United Methodist Church\n8. The ordination of women has faced opposition historically and continues to be a divisive topic within the Methodist Church.', "United Methodist Church\n9. Wesley's approach to women's ordination demonstrates a willingness to integrate women into various aspects of the church, but with limitations in terms of specific roles and settings.", 'United Methodist Church\n10. The ordination of women in the United Methodist Church has evolved over time, with changes occurring in the 20th century, but still being met with various opinions and concerns within the denomination.', "United Methodist Church\nLet's read the following passage and produce a list of implications derived directly or indirectly from the content.", 'United Methodist Church\nPassage:', 'United Methodist Church\nUnited Methodist Church', 'United Methodist Church\nThe oldest church body still in existence in the United States, the United Church of Christ (UCC), had its origins in Puritanism, though it also has its roots in Reformed theology. The Church of the United Brethren in Christ, founded in 1767, organized as a separate denomination by German-speaking Protestant immigrants from the Evangelical Association of Jurisdiction in the Eberbach, Pennsylvania area of the United States. It was founded in the former colony of Pennsylvania by followers of Bishop Peter Scheder, a student of the teachings of John Amos Comenius, also known as Jan Amos Komenský. John Amos Comenius was a Moravian educator and theologian who became known as the Father of Modern Education in the Western world and was widely popular for his missionary work and development of a universal education system throughout the world for all. The United Methodist Church came into being in 1968 as the result of a merger of the Methodist Church and the Evangelical United Brethren, both descendants of small groups of immigrant Christians that had, over the course of the next 200 years, grown to become the second and fourth largest Protestant denominations in the US. Today, the US United Methodist Church is a descendant of the original church of John Wesley and the German Brethren in Christ. As of 2017, the total membership of the UMC worldwide is 12 million, making it the second largest protestant church in the world after the Evangelical Church of Germany.', 'United Methodist Church\nImplications:', 'United Methodist Church\n1. The United Church of Christ is the oldest church body in the United States.', 'United Methodist Church\n2. The UCC has its roots in Puritanism and Reformed theology.', 'United Methodist Church\n3. The Church of the United Brethren in Christ was founded by German-speaking Protestants in Pennsylvania in 1767.', 'United Methodist Church\n4. The United Methodist Church originated as the result of a merger in 1968 between the Methodist Church and the Evangelical United Brethren, both of which had their origins in small immigrant Christian groups.', 'United Methodist Church\n5. The Methodist Church and Evangelical United Brethren were part of the second and fourth largest Protestant denominations in the US at the time of their merger.', 'United Methodist Church\n6. The United Methodist Church is a descendant of both the original church of John Wesley and the German Brethren in Christ.', 'United Methodist Church\n7. The United Methodist Church has experienced growth and expansion over the centuries, making it the second largest protestant church in the world as of 2017.', 'United Methodist Church\n8. The combined membership of the United Methodist Church worldwide is 12 million, reflecting its significant presence and influence in the global religious landscape.', 'United Methodist Church\nPrevenient grace, or the grace that "goes before" us, is given to all people. It is that power which enables us to love and motivates us to seek a relationship with God through Jesus Christ. This grace is the present work of God to turn us from our sin-corrupted human will to the loving will of the Father. In this work, God desires that we might sense both our sinfulness before God and God\'s offer of salvation. Prevenient grace allows those tainted by sin to nevertheless make a truly free choice to accept or reject God\'s salvation in Christ.'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'United Methodist Church', 'context': 'Prevenient grace, or the grace that "goes before" us, is given to all people. It is that power which enables us to love and motivates us to seek a relationship with God through Jesus Christ. This grace is the present work of God to turn us from our sin-corrupted human will to the loving will of the Father. In this work, God desires that we might sense both our sinfulness before God and God\'s offer of salvation. Prevenient grace allows those tainted by sin to nevertheless make a truly free choice to accept or reject God\'s salvation in Christ.', 'question': 'Topic: United Methodist Church\nWhat is the grace that "goes before us?"', 'answer': 'Prevenient grace'}, {'title': 'United Methodist Church', 'context': 'Prevenient grace, or the grace that "goes before" us, is given to all people. It is that power which enables us to love and motivates us to seek a relationship with God through Jesus Christ. This grace is the present work of God to turn us from our sin-corrupted human will to the loving will of the Father. In this work, God desires that we might sense both our sinfulness before God and God\'s offer of salvation. Prevenient grace allows those tainted by sin to nevertheless make a truly free choice to accept or reject God\'s salvation in Christ.', 'question': 'Topic: United Methodist Church\nWhat is that power that enables us to love?', 'answer': 'Prevenient grace'}, {'title': 'United Methodist Church', 'context': 'Prevenient grace, or the grace that "goes before" us, is given to all people. It is that power which enables us to love and motivates us to seek a relationship with God through Jesus Christ. This grace is the present work of God to turn us from our sin-corrupted human will to the loving will of the Father. In this work, God desires that we might sense both our sinfulness before God and God\'s offer of salvation. Prevenient grace allows those tainted by sin to nevertheless make a truly free choice to accept or reject God\'s salvation in Christ.', 'question': 'Topic: United Methodist Church\nWhat is Prevenient grace?', 'answer': 'the grace that "goes before" us'}, {'title': 'United Methodist Church', 'context': 'Prevenient grace, or the grace that "goes before" us, is given to all people. It is that power which enables us to love and motivates us to seek a relationship with God through Jesus Christ. This grace is the present work of God to turn us from our sin-corrupted human will to the loving will of the Father. In this work, God desires that we might sense both our sinfulness before God and God\'s offer of salvation. Prevenient grace allows those tainted by sin to nevertheless make a truly free choice to accept or reject God\'s salvation in Christ.', 'question': "Topic: United Methodist Church\nPrevenient grace allowas those tainted by what to make a choice to accept or reject God's salvation in Christ?", 'answer': 'Prevenient grace'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs': 10, 'finetune_lr': 0.001, 'batch_size': 1, 'gradient_accumulation_steps': 1, 'end_mask_substring': ''}
